        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
The primary objective of this study was to investigate the basic mechanism of how space flight affects the musculoskeletal system and immune function during long-duration missions. These accumulated baseline data were used to develop countermeasures for physiological changes in the musculoskeletal system and immune function during long space flights.
<P>
APPROACH:<br>
To evaluate the effect of a short-duration space shuttle flight on bone and bone metabolism, The Japan Aerospace Exploration Agency (JAXA) collected the pre- and post-flight data of bone mineral, bone markers, and bone-related hormones from Japanese astronauts who went to space via the space shuttle between 1994 and 2005. These medical data were obtained from five astronauts over seven space shuttle flights. The astronauts were between the ages of 32-52. Flight durations were from 8 days 21 hours 45 minutes to 15 days 16 hours 34 minutes. 
<p>
RESULTS:<br>
Body weight, lean body mass, and fat, measured by DXA body composition, decreased by 2.9%, and 3%, respectively, after a short-duration space flight. Bone mineral density (BMD), of total bone and forearm showed no change after a space shuttle flight. BMD of the skull increased by 1.4% one week after landing. BMD of the femoral neck showed very little change after landing and it decreased by 1.3% three months after landing.
Bone resorption markers, such as urinary pyridinoline, deoxypyridinoline, serum TRACP, and 1CPT, increased for one month after landing. On the other hand, bone formation makers, such as serum BAP, OC, and P1CP decreased for one week after landing, followed by an increase 1-3 months after landing. Bone-related hormones, such as PTH and CT, decreased after landing. Severe bone loss was not observed immediately after a short-duration shuttle flight. However, bone loss of the femoral neck may occur several months after landing by means of increased bone resorption and decreased bone formation during and after a short-duration space flight.
        </div>

